diff --git a/PACKAGING-UPGRADE.md b/PACKAGING-UPGRADE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..cd7e265e512bdde26e2ba8af245d6e02782e632b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/PACKAGING-UPGRADE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,103 @@
+# pg_trickle 0.81.0 to 0.92.0
+
+The package carries all 29 adjacent extension-update edges from 0.81.0 to
+0.92.0. Do not treat this as a single unattended `ALTER EXTENSION` operation.
+
+## Continuous write outage from 0.81.0 through V2 recreation
+
+The package contains one 0.92.0 shared library for every intermediate SQL
+catalog. Stop application writes and scheduling before replacing the 0.81.0
+package, and keep them stopped until the SQL catalog is 0.92.0, the V1 graph is
+dropped, the V2 graph is recreated, and consumers are resnapshotted.
+
+In particular, the 0.82.0 to 0.83.0 edge cannot rebuild dynamic CDC functions
+inside `ALTER EXTENSION` because they are intentionally not extension members.
+Do not call `rebuild_cdc_triggers()` at 0.83.0: the 0.92.0 writer references V2
+APIs and storage contracts that do not exist at that intermediate version.
+Proceed through adjacent SQL edges under the same write outage. Final DAG
+recreation at matching runtime/catalog 0.92.0 creates current V2 writers and
+fresh buffers. `rebuild_cdc_triggers()` itself is restricted to base-table
+dependencies so it never mistakes `changes_pgt_<pgt_id>` propagation buffers
+for OID-named base-table buffers.
+
+The 0.85.0 to 0.86.0 edge replaces lifecycle entry points. Packaged SQL keeps
+`create_stream_table` and `create_stream_table_if_not_exists` `CALLED ON NULL
+INPUT`, and keeps all three creation/alteration entry points `SECURITY DEFINER`,
+pinned to `pgtrickle, pg_catalog, pg_temp`, and revoked from `PUBLIC`, matching
+a fresh 0.92.0 install. These attributes are required because their optional
+defaults are NULL and the runtime validates the saved security-definer search
+path.
+
+The 0.87.15 to 0.87.16 transition changes identity-bearing stream-table state
+from a V1 `BIGINT` digest to a complete V2 `BYTEA` encoding. Existing V1
+relations are deliberately not converted in place and are rejected by the V2
+runtime.
+
+For this package, one 0.92.0 shared library accompanies every adjacent SQL
+edge. Use this tested order for the breaking boundary:
+
+1. Take a tested physical backup. Disable scheduling cluster-wide, drain
+   refreshes, stop application writes, freeze foreign producers and materialized
+   view refreshes, and hold that outage for the complete recreation window.
+   Fence new application connections at the pooler, `pg_hba.conf`, or firewall,
+   terminate and verify existing application sessions in `pg_stat_activity`, or
+   export and temporarily revoke every source-table DML ACL for exact restoration
+   later. `default_transaction_read_only` is useful for a local rehearsal but is
+   not a security boundary against a client that can change it.
+   Before package replacement, inventory user `pg_depend` entries on
+   `get_refresh_history(text,integer)`, `refresh_timeline(integer)`,
+   `st_refresh_stats()`, the five lifecycle functions replaced at 0.86.0,
+   `worker_pool_status()`, `pg_stat_stream_tables`, and `stream_tables_info`.
+   Also export function
+   owner/config/comment metadata and the two views' owner, comment,
+   `reloptions`, relation and column ACLs. The relevant edges use
+   `DROP ... RESTRICT`; non-default owner/config/comment/view metadata is a
+   deliberate fail-closed condition. Resolve and explicitly restore such state
+   rather than using `CASCADE`.
+2. Install the 0.92.0 package, restart PostgreSQL, and run adjacent SQL edges
+   through 0.87.15 without executing intermediate DML or CDC writers.
+3. Advance to 0.87.16 and verify that V1 state fails closed without changing
+   source or stream data. Advance to 0.87.17, register every
+   logical-replication, publication, outbox, DuckLake, dbt, and user-SQL
+   consumer and acknowledge the inventory itself. Keep every individual
+   consumer `PENDING` or `IN_PROGRESS` and unacknowledged until its post-V2
+   resnapshot is complete. Export the DAG with the downstream-provided
+   `pg_trickle_dump` and `pg_dump`. The recreation preflight must report both
+   the still-present V1 graph and non-terminal consumers as incompatible.
+4. Keep writes and scheduling paused and advance the SQL catalog through
+   0.88.0, 0.89.0, 0.90.0, 0.91.0, and 0.92.0. Do not call stream-table
+   lifecycle APIs while the 0.92.0 runtime and an intermediate SQL catalog
+   differ. The 0.92.0 runtime's drop path requires catalog objects introduced
+   by 0.89.0.
+5. With runtime and SQL catalog both at 0.92.0, detach exported external
+   dependents and drop V1 stream tables in reverse dependency order. Never
+   rewrite or cast V1 identifiers, and never reuse old change buffers. Verify
+   that source tables remain and the old graph is absent. The preflight is
+   still expected to fail because consumers have not yet been resnapshotted.
+   The 0.81 MATVIEW/FOREIGN polling paths can leave an unused V1 buffer and
+   `pgt_change_tracking` row after the final stream-table drop. Record their
+   keys before cleanup. Delete a row only after proving
+   `pgt_stream_tables` is empty, no `pgt_dependencies` row references its source
+   or stream ID, and any matching registry markers are both NULL. Drop only the
+   exact buffer and snapshot tables without `CASCADE`, delete only those exact
+   registry/tracking keys, and record the empty after-state. A missing buffer is
+   acceptable only with the same no-stream/no-dependency proof.
+6. Replay the exported definitions in dependency order, perform fresh initial
+   refreshes, restore intended ACLs and comments, change every external
+   consumer identity column to `BYTEA`, resnapshot, and only then acknowledge
+   `COMPLETE` or an explicitly reviewed `SKIPPED`. Require the recreation
+   preflight to pass on the new V2 graph. Assert that every new dependency has
+   a matching V2 `(row_identity_version,row_probe_version)=(2,1)` buffer and
+   tracking entry, with no orphaned V1 key.
+7. Keep the capture instance quiesced until `preflight_upgrade()` and
+   `validate_recovery()` pass. A restored or cloned database whose capture
+   identity changed remains quarantined; use the superuser-only
+   `recover_capture_instance()` workflow only for an intentional adoption.
+   Re-enable writes and scheduling only after every resnapshot, then verify the
+   loaded library version, `pg_extension.extversion`, source and derived data,
+   restart, and a tested dump/restore.
+
+Writes made during DAG recreation are not replayed. Keep the scheduler disabled
+until every affected stream table and external consumer has been recreated and
+resnapshotted. Consult the v0.92.0 upstream `docs/UPGRADING.md` for the complete
+preflight, inventory, rollback, and privacy requirements.
